_gate_piotroski":5,"_gp_piotroski":false,"_score_piotroski":55.6,"_gate_spread":null,"_gp_spread":null,"_score_spread":0.0,"_gate_roic_consistency":null,"_gp_roic_consistency":null,"_score_roic_consistency":0.0,"_gate_incr_roic":null,"_gp_incr_roic":null,"_score_incr_roic":0.0,"_gate_margin_advantage":-0.11948955650290821,"_gp_margin_advantage":false,"_score_margin_advantage":0.0,"_gate_pool_share":-0.24981559860987668,"_gp_pool_share":false,"_score_pool_share":0.0,"_gate_rev_durability":0.04054859577121417,"_gp_rev_durability":true,"_score_rev_durability":45.3,"_gate_fcf_durability":0.10224125828994657,"_gp_fcf_durability":true,"_score_fcf_durability":76.1,"_gate_margins":0.010698885266702797,"_gp_margins":true,"_score_margins":60.7,"_gate_fund_growth":null,"_gp_fund_growth":null,"_score_fund_growth":0.0,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.06577029330474604,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":true,"_score_shrhldr_yield":84.2,"_gate_share_shrink":null,"_gp_share_shrink":null,"_score_share_shrink":0.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.0007539383357803262,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":98.7,"_gate_insider_own":0.21312,"_gp_insider_own":true,"_score_insider_own":100.0,"_gate_insider_buying":null,"_gp_insider_buying":null,"_score_insider_buying":null,"pf":[],"description_full":"Sunoco LP, together with its subsidiaries, engages in the energy infrastructure and distribution of motor fuels in the United States. It operates in four segments: Fuel Distribution, Pipeline Systems, Refinery, and Terminals. The Fuel Distribution segment distributes motor fuels and other petroleum products, such as propane and lubricating oil to third-party dealers and distributors, independent operators of commission agent locations, other commercial consumers of motor fuel, and retail locations; and leases real estate properties. This segment also offers non-fuel products, including in-store merchandise and company-operated retail stores food services, as well as credit card processing, car washes, lottery, and other services. The Pipeline Systems segment includes an integrated pipeline and terminal network comprising refined product, crude oil, and ammonia pipelines and terminals. The Terminals segment operates transmix processing facilities and refined product terminals; and provides blending, additive injections, handling, and filtering services. The company was formerly known as Susser Petroleum Partners LP and changed its name to Sunoco LP in 2014. Sunoco LP was founded in 1960 and is based in Dallas, Texas.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Joseph  Kim | President, CEO & Director of Sunoco GP LLC | Age 53 | Compensation: $2.3M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 8K people. Revenue per employee of $2.8M ranks in the above the sector median among Energy peers, improving at 15%/yr \u2014 growing workforce leverage. Each employee generates $69K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $2K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 1\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity).","financial_summary":["Sunoco LP is a large-cap energy business with a $11.6B market cap on $25.2B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -1% over the past three years and +4% annually across the full decade \u2014 essentially flat, and a business treading water is rarely a path to compounding.","Gross margins of 8% are the starting point for assessing pricing power.","The business throws off $615M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 2 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 2.26\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 7.7\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin 1.7\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Management returns a meaningful 6.6% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insiders hold 21% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is, and profit capture is only 0.3\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 1152% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 1 other valuation measure)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":11.52459749216302},{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (1.4) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Operating margins 12% below sector median \u2014 a cost disadvantage the moat cannot hide","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Value-trap profile (score 53/100): leveraged balance sheet under pressure; roic below cost of capital \u2014 cheapness may reflect deterioration, not mispricing","sev":"amber","cat":"value_trap"},{"text":"Carries more leverage than most Energy peers \u2014 a smaller cushion for the inevitable bad year","sev":"amber","cat":"balance_sheet"},{"text":"Cyclical sectors like Energy tend to underperform in slowdowns","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Above-average market volatility (VIX 26)","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Late-cycle dynamics increase the probability of a slowdown","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Geopolitical supply shocks \u2014 wars, sanctions, and OPEC decisions create volatility that no hedging programme can fully neutralise.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["13 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible","Energy sector showing strong relative momentum, outperforming the market by 6% over 3 months","Energy ETF in a sustained uptrend above its long-term average"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"How Much Did Natural Hazards Cost Suncorp (ASX:SUN)?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/au/stocks/financial/how-much-did-natural-hazards-cost-suncorp-asxsun","date":"2026-11-06","timestamp":1793928240.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.655,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Insurance Focus: Can ASX 50's Suncorp (ASX:SUN) Keep Its Dividend Strong?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/au/stocks/dividend/insurance-focus-can-asx-50s-suncorp-asxsun-keep-its-dividend-strong","date":"2026-11-05","timestamp":1793855760.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":-0.128,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["dividend"]},{"title":"ASX 100 insurance stocks: Will reinsurance steady Suncorp (ASX:SUN) after storm overruns?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/au/stocks/financial/asx-100-insurance-stocks-will-reinsurance-steady-suncorp-asxsun-after-storm-overruns","date":"2026-11-05","timestamp":1793852820.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.511,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Sunoco (NYSE:SUN) Announces Quarterly Earnings Results","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/04/sunoco-nysesun-announces-quarterly-earnings-results.html","date":"2026-11-04","timestamp":1793810456.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.273,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["earnings"]},{"title":"Weather Bill, New Buy-Back: What's Next for ASX 50's Suncorp (ASX:SUN)?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/au/stocks/financial/weather-bill-new-buy-back-whats-next-for-asx-50s-suncorp-asxsun","date":"2026-11-04","timestamp":1793783340.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.238,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Premium Pullback: Why Is Suncorp (ASX:SUN) Trimming Its Growth Outlook?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/au/stocks/dividend/premium-pullback-why-is-sunc